## The one mistake that fails silently

`prompt` is inspected by the guardrail. `system` is not.

Tool output and user questions go in `prompt`. Instructions this service wrote
go in `system`. Putting tool output in `system` routes it around the platform's
injection defence and you get a 200 and a plausible answer — nothing in a
passing test run reveals it (ADR-013).

## Local

```
make check                                          # repo root: lint, tests, synth
pave eval --dataset services/{{ name }}/eval --dry-run   # the plan, no model calls
pave eval --dataset services/{{ name }}/eval             # needs AWS; costs money
```

`eval/golden.yaml` seeds five cases over recorded fixtures. Add to it before
you add capabilities — a service whose dataset lags its features has a gate
that grades a subset and reports a whole.

## Turning the judge on

Every seed case is `deterministic`. That is deliberate, not an oversight.

A judged case needs a **calibrated** judge, and calibration needs answers a
person labelled by hand. `{{ name }}` has never run, so no such answers exist
and none can be invented — a "hand-labelled" sample nobody labelled is a number
the gate would then trust to grade real work.

To turn it on, in this order:

1. `pave eval --dataset services/{{ name }}/eval` and keep the answers.
2. Label ten of them in `eval/calibration.yaml` — `human_pass` plus a `note`
   saying *why*. Aim for a rough balance of passes and failures; ten samples
   you all agree with measure nothing.
3. Switch the cases you want judged to `grading: judged`.

The loader refuses a judged case when there are no calibration samples, so this
cannot be left half-done. Agreement below 80% fails the run rather than
downgrading it.
